1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,880
How rich is Elon Musk?

2
00:00:02,220 --> 00:00:06,980
Imagine walking into an Apple Store and
asking for an iPhone 18 Pro.

3
00:00:07,640 --> 00:00:11,340
Then another. Then enough to turn the
entire store into a rounding

4
00:00:11,340 --> 00:00:15,793
error. Using a Forbes estimate of his
fortune from October twenty-third,

5
00:00:15,793 --> 00:00:20,380
twenty twenty-six, and Apple's American
starting price, his net

6
00:00:20,380 --> 00:00:24,040
worth was equivalent to roughly seven
hundred seventy million of those

7
00:00:24,040 --> 00:00:29,760
phones. On paper. Put one phone through
the checkout every second, without

8
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:33,280
stopping to sleep, and you would still be
there more than twenty-four

9
00:00:33,280 --> 00:00:34,160
years later.

10
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:36,040
The cashier would retire.

11
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:38,740
The replacement cashier might retire.

12
00:00:39,420 --> 00:00:42,820
You would still be buying phones that were
already getting very old.

13
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:48,020
And yet, if Musk actually arrived with
that shopping cart, the most

14
00:00:48,020 --> 00:00:50,680
important question would be surprisingly
ordinary.

15
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:52,440
How are you paying?

16
00:00:53,320 --> 00:00:56,660
Because the number on a billionaire list
and the money available at a

17
00:00:56,660 --> 00:01:00,740
checkout are connected in a way that
headlines rarely have time to

18
00:01:00,740 --> 00:01:06,160
explain. To understand how rich he really
is, we need to do two things:

19
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:10,440
make the number feel real, and figure out
what that number actually

20
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:13,920
means. First, our receipt needs a date.

21
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:17,180
For this episode, we are using one
published snapshot:

22
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,000
an estimated nine hundred twenty-two point
nine billion dollars.

23
00:01:22,440 --> 00:01:23,320
We will freeze it there.

24
00:01:23,940 --> 00:01:27,820
By the time you watch this, markets may
have moved and the ranking may

25
00:01:27,820 --> 00:01:28,740
show something else.

26
00:01:29,460 --> 00:01:31,900
Different trackers can also produce
different estimates.

27
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:37,460
For the phone, we are using the iPhone 18
Pro, starting at

28
00:01:37,460 --> 00:01:40,360
eleven hundred ninety-nine dollars in the
United States.

29
00:01:41,240 --> 00:01:43,600
No trade-in. No installment plan.

30
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:46,300
No sales tax added to our comparison.

31
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:51,080
That gives us a simple division: the
wealth estimate divided by the

32
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:51,660
phone price.

33
00:01:52,640 --> 00:01:56,660
The result is about seven hundred
sixty-nine point seven million phones,

34
00:01:57,200 --> 00:01:59,500
which we round to seven hundred seventy
million.

35
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:02,600
This is a way to visualize a financial
estimate.

36
00:02:03,380 --> 00:02:06,580
It assumes the quoted wealth could be
turned into spending money at its

37
00:02:06,580 --> 00:02:10,780
stated value and that every phone remained
available at the same price.

38
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:13,560
Those assumptions are doing a lot of work.

39
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:17,260
For now, let the cartoon shopping cart
keep growing.

40
00:02:17,860 --> 00:02:18,960
We will return to the checkout.

41
00:02:18,960 --> 00:02:21,960
A million, a billion, a trillion.

42
00:02:22,660 --> 00:02:26,240
They sound like three neighboring houses
on the same expensive street.

43
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:28,720
The distances between them are enormous.

44
00:02:29,860 --> 00:02:31,480
One billion is a thousand million.

45
00:02:32,260 --> 00:02:34,080
One trillion is a thousand billion.

46
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:37,580
Imagine a clock counting one dollar every
second.

47
00:02:38,260 --> 00:02:41,560
To count a million dollars takes about
eleven and a half days.

48
00:02:42,300 --> 00:02:46,140
To count a billion takes about thirty-one
point seven years.

49
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:50,620
To count a trillion takes roughly
thirty-one thousand seven hundred

50
00:02:50,620 --> 00:02:53,740
years. The clock has not become slower.

51
00:02:54,440 --> 00:02:58,080
We have just reached numbers that ordinary
experience does a terrible

52
00:02:58,080 --> 00:02:59,220
job of imagining.

53
00:03:00,040 --> 00:03:02,360
Now return to our selected estimate.

54
00:03:03,080 --> 00:03:05,900
It sits much closer to one trillion than
to one billion.

55
00:03:06,640 --> 00:03:09,720
A picture of a slightly bigger wallet
cannot show that.

56
00:03:09,720 --> 00:03:12,620
Neither can a mansion with a few extra
windows.

57
00:03:13,500 --> 00:03:15,540
We need the picture to change scale
completely:

58
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:20,960
from a handful of phone boxes, to rows, to
warehouses, to a counter

59
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:22,500
that refuses to stop.

60
00:03:23,480 --> 00:03:26,720
That is what the iPhone comparison is
useful for.

61
00:03:27,240 --> 00:03:30,580
It translates an abstract number into an
object you recognize,

62
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:35,680
then reveals how quickly that object stops
being a useful container for

63
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:36,080
the scale.

64
00:03:37,080 --> 00:03:40,660
Let's abandon the phones and give our
imaginary shopper a different

65
00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:43,920
challenge. Spend one million dollars every
day.

66
00:03:44,520 --> 00:03:45,680
Every morning, a million.

67
00:03:46,360 --> 00:03:49,480
Every afternoon, another plan for
tomorrow's million.

68
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:54,540
Weekends included. Starting from our
frozen wealth estimate, that simple

69
00:03:54,540 --> 00:03:58,700
subtraction exercise lasts more than two
thousand five hundred years.

70
00:03:59,620 --> 00:04:01,760
This assumes no investment returns,

71
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:03,140
no inflation,

72
00:04:03,880 --> 00:04:08,020
no taxes, and no changes in the value of
the underlying assets.

73
00:04:08,820 --> 00:04:12,600
It also assumes the entire amount was
already spendable cash.

74
00:04:13,420 --> 00:04:17,960
It is a deliberately unrealistic
stopwatch, useful for understanding

75
00:04:17,960 --> 00:04:23,700
size. Try another one. Give a fictional
person sixty thousand dollars a year

76
00:04:23,700 --> 00:04:25,120
and let them save every cent.

77
00:04:25,880 --> 00:04:27,280
No rent. No groceries.

78
00:04:28,020 --> 00:04:28,620
No taxes.

79
00:04:29,420 --> 00:04:31,040
Absolutely no phone upgrades.

80
00:04:31,980 --> 00:04:35,520
Reaching one billion dollars that way
takes about sixteen thousand seven

81
00:04:35,520 --> 00:04:36,540
hundred years.

82
00:04:37,340 --> 00:04:40,920
Reaching our selected estimate takes more
than fifteen million years.

83
00:04:41,910 --> 00:04:44,620
That is not a realistic financial plan.

84
00:04:45,320 --> 00:04:48,960
The assumptions remove the person's entire
life to make the arithmetic

85
00:04:48,960 --> 00:04:52,780
simple. But the comparison reveals
something important:

86
00:04:53,560 --> 00:04:58,120
a fortune at this scale is difficult to
explain as a very large pile of

87
00:04:58,120 --> 00:05:01,660
ordinary annual paychecks. We need to look
at ownership.

88
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:07,060
When someone says a person is worth a
particular amount, the basic idea

89
00:05:07,060 --> 00:05:11,540
is net worth: the estimated value of their
assets, minus their

90
00:05:11,540 --> 00:05:17,220
liabilities. Assets can include money in
an account, investments, property, and

91
00:05:17,220 --> 00:05:17,920
business interests.

92
00:05:18,840 --> 00:05:22,620
Liabilities are financial obligations owed
to other people.

93
00:05:23,620 --> 00:05:25,080
Imagine a fictional homeowner.

94
00:05:25,920 --> 00:05:29,420
Their house is valued at five hundred
thousand dollars.

95
00:05:29,420 --> 00:05:32,580
Their mortgage balance is three hundred
thousand.

96
00:05:32,580 --> 00:05:35,600
The difference is two hundred thousand
dollars of home equity.

97
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:37,800
That person has something valuable.

98
00:05:38,660 --> 00:05:41,960
They cannot necessarily hand a cashier two
hundred thousand dollars

99
00:05:41,960 --> 00:05:44,860
tomorrow while keeping everything else
unchanged.

100
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:50,160
They may have to sell the property, borrow
against it, or use other

101
00:05:50,160 --> 00:05:53,080
money. Each route has conditions and
consequences.

102
00:05:54,020 --> 00:05:55,880
Now enlarge the idea dramatically.

103
00:05:56,880 --> 00:06:01,420
The Forbes snapshot identifies Tesla and
SpaceX as major sources

104
00:06:01,420 --> 00:06:05,900
of Musk's fortune. A great deal of the
question concerns the value of

105
00:06:05,900 --> 00:06:09,840
business ownership, rather than a single
account containing the entire

106
00:06:09,840 --> 00:06:10,960
headline number.

107
00:06:12,040 --> 00:06:14,620
We are not claiming to know his personal
cash balance.

108
00:06:15,460 --> 00:06:18,420
The billionaire ranking does not answer
that question for us.

109
00:06:19,080 --> 00:06:22,220
The same distinction matters even when the
owner is extraordinarily

110
00:06:22,220 --> 00:06:28,340
wealthy: what you own, what it is valued
at, what you owe, and what you can spend

111
00:06:28,340 --> 00:06:31,500
immediately are different entries on the
financial map.

112
00:06:32,200 --> 00:06:36,100
Let's build a fictional company small
enough to draw on one sheet of

113
00:06:36,100 --> 00:06:38,860
paper. It has one thousand shares
outstanding.

114
00:06:39,820 --> 00:06:41,620
Someone owns two hundred of them.

115
00:06:42,180 --> 00:06:46,420
If the market price is one hundred dollars
per share, multiplying that

116
00:06:46,420 --> 00:06:50,820
price by all one thousand shares gives a
market capitalization of

117
00:06:50,820 --> 00:06:52,840
one hundred thousand dollars.

118
00:06:52,840 --> 00:06:57,200
At that price, the owner's two hundred
shares are valued at

119
00:06:57,200 --> 00:07:01,970
twenty thousand dollars. Now suppose the
market price rises to one

120
00:07:01,970 --> 00:07:06,376
hundred fifty dollars. The company's
market capitalization becomes one

121
00:07:06,376 --> 00:07:08,760
hundred fifty thousand dollars.

122
00:07:08,760 --> 00:07:13,070
The same person's stake is now valued at
thirty thousand.

123
00:07:13,070 --> 00:07:17,040
Their estimated wealth from that stake has
increased by ten thousand

124
00:07:17,040 --> 00:07:20,980
dollars. We did not show a customer
sending ten thousand dollars into

125
00:07:20,980 --> 00:07:22,340
the owner's checking account.

126
00:07:23,120 --> 00:07:25,460
We changed the price used to value their
shares.

127
00:07:26,360 --> 00:07:30,080
That change can represent a meaningful
change in what people are willing

128
00:07:30,080 --> 00:07:34,160
to pay. It still tells us something
different from a cash payment the

129
00:07:34,160 --> 00:07:35,720
owner has already received.

130
00:07:36,720 --> 00:07:41,100
At enormous ownership scales, even a
modest percentage change can

131
00:07:41,100 --> 00:07:43,880
produce a spectacular headline change in
dollars.

132
00:07:44,720 --> 00:07:47,560
The number gets attention because it moves
dramatically.

133
00:07:48,480 --> 00:07:52,560
Understanding the movement requires
knowing which price, which asset,

134
00:07:53,060 --> 00:07:55,740
and which ownership interest moved
underneath it.

135
00:07:56,500 --> 00:08:00,380
To see the scale, take one percent of our
frozen headline estimate.

136
00:08:01,180 --> 00:08:04,620
That is about nine point two three billion
dollars.

137
00:08:04,620 --> 00:08:08,700
Expressed in our phone unit, that amount
alone is equivalent to roughly

138
00:08:08,700 --> 00:08:13,200
seven point seven million iPhone 18 Pros
at the same

139
00:08:13,200 --> 00:08:14,120
starting price.

140
00:08:15,220 --> 00:08:17,640
This is just one percent of the selected
estimate.

141
00:08:18,460 --> 00:08:22,520
We are not predicting a market move,
saying all of Musk's assets move

142
00:08:22,520 --> 00:08:25,840
together, or claiming he received that
amount as income.

143
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:30,340
We are showing why a percentage that
sounds small can become an

144
00:08:30,340 --> 00:08:33,140
extraordinary number when the starting
amount is huge.

145
00:08:34,380 --> 00:08:37,420
It also explains a common mistake in
wealth stories.

146
00:08:38,220 --> 00:08:40,880
Take a change in estimated asset value.

147
00:08:41,540 --> 00:08:43,840
Divide it by the number of hours in a day.

148
00:08:44,560 --> 00:08:47,100
Put a dramatic amount next to the words
per hour.

149
00:08:47,720 --> 00:08:51,340
The arithmetic may be correct while the
suggested mental picture is

150
00:08:51,340 --> 00:08:56,620
wrong. It can make a repricing of assets
sound like someone was paid an hourly

151
00:08:56,620 --> 00:09:02,140
wage, or make an average over one chosen
period sound like a reliable future

152
00:09:02,140 --> 00:09:03,160
income stream.

153
00:09:03,920 --> 00:09:07,620
Before being impressed by a billionaire's
supposed earnings per second,

154
00:09:07,960 --> 00:09:12,480
check what was divided, over what period,
and whether anyone actually

155
00:09:12,480 --> 00:09:16,320
received a payment. This brings us back to
the cashier.

156
00:09:16,820 --> 00:09:19,080
Suppose our fictional billionaire says,
fine,

157
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:22,080
I will sell the assets and come back with
cash.

158
00:09:22,820 --> 00:09:24,880
That can be a real route to spending
money.

159
00:09:25,660 --> 00:09:29,680
The practical question is how much can be
sold, how quickly, on what

160
00:09:29,680 --> 00:09:30,900
terms, and to whom.

161
00:09:32,060 --> 00:09:36,160
A quoted market price is not a signed
promise from enough buyers to take

162
00:09:36,160 --> 00:09:39,240
an owner's entire stake at that exact
price.

163
00:09:39,240 --> 00:09:43,860
Imagine a small market with several buyers
offering to purchase a few

164
00:09:43,860 --> 00:09:46,000
shares at one hundred dollars.

165
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:47,920
You can see that price on a screen.

166
00:09:48,980 --> 00:09:51,900
Now arrive with a much larger quantity to
sell.

167
00:09:52,640 --> 00:09:55,860
The buyers available at the first price
may not want all of it.

168
00:09:56,540 --> 00:09:59,420
Completing the sale may require finding
more buyers,

169
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:03,160
accepting other prices, or taking more
time.

170
00:10:03,160 --> 00:10:05,740
That is part of the problem of liquidity:

171
00:10:06,460 --> 00:10:09,760
how readily an asset can be traded at an
acceptable price.

172
00:10:10,960 --> 00:10:12,700
Large sales can affect prices.

173
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:17,480
Restrictions, transaction costs, and taxes
may also matter.

174
00:10:18,280 --> 00:10:22,220
None of that means a wealthy owner can
never sell, or that the assets

175
00:10:22,220 --> 00:10:22,900
are worthless.

176
00:10:23,720 --> 00:10:28,080
It means that multiplying a quoted value
across a large holding and

177
00:10:28,080 --> 00:10:31,480
turning the entire result into cash are
different operations.

178
00:10:32,300 --> 00:10:35,880
The headline estimate is a useful view of
a fortune.

179
00:10:36,640 --> 00:10:38,760
It is not a guaranteed liquidation
receipt.

180
00:10:40,240 --> 00:10:44,420
There is another possible route from an
asset to spending power: a loan

181
00:10:44,420 --> 00:10:46,320
secured against something valuable.

182
00:10:47,300 --> 00:10:51,280
A lender may be willing to advance money
using eligible assets as

183
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:56,580
collateral. The owner receives cash while
taking on a liability and agreeing to

184
00:10:56,580 --> 00:11:01,680
terms. For some borrowers, that can make
assets useful without an immediate

185
00:11:01,680 --> 00:11:05,160
sale. The loan still has to be serviced or
repaid.

186
00:11:06,040 --> 00:11:07,800
Interest, collateral requirements,

187
00:11:08,520 --> 00:11:10,680
and the risk of falling asset values
matter.

188
00:11:11,360 --> 00:11:15,080
In securities-backed borrowing
arrangements, a decline in collateral

189
00:11:15,080 --> 00:11:18,580
value can create demands for more
collateral or repayment.

190
00:11:19,500 --> 00:11:21,760
Some arrangements can lead to forced
sales.

191
00:11:22,720 --> 00:11:27,280
We are explaining a general financial
mechanism, not reporting an

192
00:11:27,280 --> 00:11:31,400
undisclosed Musk loan or recommending a
borrowing strategy.

193
00:11:32,180 --> 00:11:36,340
The point is that liquidity constraints
and financial power can coexist.

194
00:11:37,160 --> 00:11:41,420
A fortune can be difficult to spend in one
enormous transaction while

195
00:11:41,420 --> 00:11:44,520
still providing substantial options for
raising money.

196
00:11:45,260 --> 00:11:49,420
Saying an owner does not hold the whole
fortune in cash should not lead

197
00:11:49,420 --> 00:11:53,040
us to pretend that ownership provides no
financial advantage.

198
00:11:53,780 --> 00:11:54,720
It provides options.

199
00:11:55,520 --> 00:11:58,360
Those options have terms, limits, and
risks.

200
00:11:59,240 --> 00:12:02,560
Our shopping cart has found another
possible entrance to the store.

201
00:12:03,180 --> 00:12:05,200
It has not found a way to make debt
disappear.

202
00:12:06,200 --> 00:12:08,740
We also need to separate the owner from
the businesses.

203
00:12:08,740 --> 00:12:13,120
A company's money, buildings, equipment,
and obligations belong

204
00:12:13,120 --> 00:12:15,040
on its own financial statements.

205
00:12:15,960 --> 00:12:19,780
A shareholder owns a stake in the company,
with rights attached to that

206
00:12:19,780 --> 00:12:23,480
stake. The company may need cash for
wages, suppliers,

207
00:12:24,200 --> 00:12:26,340
equipment, debt, and other operations.

208
00:12:27,200 --> 00:12:30,780
Its spending decisions also sit within
corporate rules and obligations.

209
00:12:31,520 --> 00:12:36,100
A large personal wealth estimate does not
make all of a company's bank

210
00:12:36,100 --> 00:12:38,680
accounts the owner's personal spending
account.

211
00:12:39,400 --> 00:12:43,640
Ownership can carry voting rights and
influence, depending on the shares

212
00:12:43,640 --> 00:12:44,740
and governance arrangements.

213
00:12:45,680 --> 00:12:47,380
That can be extremely consequential.

214
00:12:48,360 --> 00:12:53,080
It does not make personal cash, corporate
cash, ownership value, and

215
00:12:53,080 --> 00:12:55,100
managerial authority interchangeable.

216
00:12:55,820 --> 00:12:58,540
For our drawing, give each a separate box.

217
00:12:59,300 --> 00:13:01,340
One box is the person's spending money.

218
00:13:02,260 --> 00:13:03,860
Another contains their ownership
interests.

219
00:13:04,940 --> 00:13:07,380
A third belongs to the business and its
operations.

220
00:13:08,500 --> 00:13:10,620
A fourth shows obligations and rules.

221
00:13:11,580 --> 00:13:15,080
The story becomes much clearer when we
keep the boxes separate and

222
00:13:15,080 --> 00:13:16,880
explain the connections between them.

223
00:13:17,640 --> 00:13:22,240
Otherwise, the animation quietly turns an
entire working organization

224
00:13:22,240 --> 00:13:25,640
into a wallet with one person's name
printed on it.

225
00:13:26,600 --> 00:13:29,800
At this point, the comparisons usually
become even bigger.

226
00:13:30,680 --> 00:13:31,400
More than a country.

227
00:13:31,840 --> 00:13:33,300
Enough to own an economy.

228
00:13:34,120 --> 00:13:36,800
A personal fortune larger than a nation's
GDP.

229
00:13:38,060 --> 00:13:41,740
We need to inspect the units before
turning that into a map with a price

230
00:13:41,740 --> 00:13:46,020
sticker. GDP measures production over a
period.

231
00:13:46,820 --> 00:13:51,000
A personal wealth estimate measures a
stock of assets and liabilities at

232
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:51,840
a point in time.

233
00:13:52,660 --> 00:13:56,760
Comparing the numbers can illustrate
scale, provided we say what they

234
00:13:56,760 --> 00:14:00,580
measure. It does not tell us what a
country would cost to purchase.

235
00:14:01,740 --> 00:14:06,040
Annual economic output is not a sale price
for its land, institutions,

236
00:14:06,900 --> 00:14:09,380
businesses, resources, or people.

237
00:14:09,380 --> 00:14:13,560
There is a similar problem when we say a
fortune could fund a particular

238
00:14:13,560 --> 00:14:18,100
number of houses, hospitals, or schools
without explaining the

239
00:14:18,100 --> 00:14:18,780
cost assumptions.

240
00:14:20,220 --> 00:14:22,960
A funding amount can be divided by an
assumed cost.

241
00:14:23,900 --> 00:14:27,660
Real construction and services require
suitable land, workers,

242
00:14:28,080 --> 00:14:30,740
materials, approvals, and ongoing
resources.

243
00:14:31,620 --> 00:14:33,180
Costs can vary substantially.

244
00:14:34,160 --> 00:14:37,580
That makes the spending question more
interesting, rather than less

245
00:14:37,580 --> 00:14:43,420
important. Instead of asking only how many
things fit inside the number, we can

246
00:14:43,420 --> 00:14:45,880
ask what resources the money could
mobilize,

247
00:14:46,580 --> 00:14:47,360
under which constraints,

248
00:14:48,180 --> 00:14:49,940
and with what continuing commitments.

249
00:14:51,080 --> 00:14:53,480
Here is a simple hypothetical project.

250
00:14:54,260 --> 00:14:56,560
Set aside one billion dollars.

251
00:14:56,560 --> 00:15:01,145
Assume, purely for this example, an all-in
cost of one hundred thousand

252
00:15:01,145 --> 00:15:05,900
dollars per worker-year. The division
gives ten thousand worker-years of

253
00:15:05,900 --> 00:15:10,900
funding. That could mean one thousand
people for ten years under the same fixed

254
00:15:10,900 --> 00:15:11,640
cost assumption.

255
00:15:12,540 --> 00:15:13,900
It is a labor-budget illustration,

256
00:15:14,820 --> 00:15:16,980
not a promise about hiring, salaries,

257
00:15:17,840 --> 00:15:19,360
or the total cost of a project.

258
00:15:20,200 --> 00:15:24,360
Equipment, premises, changing costs, and
other needs could require

259
00:15:24,360 --> 00:15:25,520
additional funding.

260
00:15:26,560 --> 00:15:28,320
But notice how the picture has changed.

261
00:15:28,320 --> 00:15:33,100
We have moved from one person surrounded
by expensive objects to

262
00:15:33,100 --> 00:15:36,440
the ability to support people working
toward a goal over time.

263
00:15:37,240 --> 00:15:41,400
At very large scales, wealth can influence
which ventures get attempted,

264
00:15:42,040 --> 00:15:46,400
which teams receive backing, which risks
someone can absorb, and how

265
00:15:46,400 --> 00:15:47,780
long they can keep trying.

266
00:15:48,820 --> 00:15:51,180
Those choices do not guarantee a good
result.

267
00:15:51,180 --> 00:15:55,740
Money does not supply every skill, solve
every coordination problem,

268
00:15:56,360 --> 00:15:58,820
or remove every legal and physical
constraint.

269
00:15:59,620 --> 00:16:02,640
It does create room to act that many
people do not have.

270
00:16:03,200 --> 00:16:06,840
That is part of what gets lost when the
entire conversation becomes an

271
00:16:06,840 --> 00:16:09,420
endless list of yachts, phones, and
houses.

272
00:16:10,340 --> 00:16:11,780
The objects make wealth visible.

273
00:16:12,600 --> 00:16:14,560
The options help explain its significance.

274
00:16:15,440 --> 00:16:17,960
So, how rich is Elon Musk?

275
00:16:18,380 --> 00:16:22,560
Using our dated snapshot and selected
phone price, the answer is

276
00:16:22,560 --> 00:16:27,060
equivalent to about seven hundred seventy
million iPhone 18 Pros

277
00:16:27,060 --> 00:16:29,540
before the costs and constraints we set
aside.

278
00:16:30,420 --> 00:16:31,460
That gives us the scale.

279
00:16:32,600 --> 00:16:36,480
To understand the fortune, we also need
its composition, liabilities,

280
00:16:37,600 --> 00:16:41,840
changing asset values, liquidity, and the
practical ways ownership can

281
00:16:41,840 --> 00:16:43,780
become spending power or influence.

282
00:16:43,780 --> 00:16:48,240
A falling valuation can reduce an owner's
estimated wealth

283
00:16:48,240 --> 00:16:51,720
without the same amount being withdrawn
from their bank account.

284
00:16:52,580 --> 00:16:56,800
A rising valuation can increase it without
an identical cash deposit.

285
00:16:58,300 --> 00:17:02,720
And a large fortune can provide
substantial power and options even when

286
00:17:02,720 --> 00:17:06,220
the owner cannot instantly spend the
entire published estimate.

287
00:17:07,120 --> 00:17:10,360
Our imaginary shopper finally reaches the
front of the line.

288
00:17:10,360 --> 00:17:13,140
The cashier looks at the mountain of
phones,

289
00:17:13,920 --> 00:17:14,560
then at the screen,

290
00:17:15,260 --> 00:17:17,240
then asks the question we started with.

291
00:17:18,220 --> 00:17:19,000
How are you paying?

292
00:17:19,660 --> 00:17:22,080
The answer needs more than a number on a
rich list.

293
00:17:22,080 --> 00:17:26,760
Because extreme wealth is easier to count
in familiar objects than it is

294
00:17:26,760 --> 00:17:27,260
to understand.

295
00:17:28,240 --> 00:17:30,360
The phone pile is where this story begins.

296
00:17:31,340 --> 00:17:35,140
The assets, obligations, and choices
behind it are what make the story

297
00:17:35,140 --> 00:17:37,480
matter. This is Scopivo.

298
00:17:37,480 --> 00:17:38,360
A clearer view.
